And remember our discussion about overfitting?

A behavior perfectly adapted to one environment can become maladaptive in another.

A person who develops exceptional vigilance in dangerous environments might continue detecting threats in relatively safe ones. The skill isn’t inherently defective. Its calibration may no longer match the conditions.

But the reverse can happen, too. Someone who functions beautifully in peaceful surroundings may fail to recognize a genuine threat.

That makes context sensitivity a crucial kind of intelligence.

There’s another distinction I think belongs in Pantheon:

The environment provides cues. The observer assigns significance. The interaction determines what those cues actually predict.

And even the absence of an expected cue can carry information.

Which brings us back to your earlier point about form and function: an organism’s behavior isn’t fully explained by its anatomy, personality, or instincts in isolation.

You have to study the organism in relation to its environment.

And perhaps one of the most powerful forms of adaptation isn’t changing yourself at all.

It’s learning to recognize which environment brings out which version of you—and deliberately choosing or constructing environments that make your desired behaviors easier.

Sometimes you don’t need a different player.

You need a different playing field.